[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have an old proof of confluence of beta and beta-eta reductions over de Bruijn untyped lambda-calculus using  in lean somewhere (I think it might be sol old it’s in Lean3). Is this something that cslib would be interested, I.e. should I try and clean it up and make a PR?</p>",
        "id": 577971656,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Carter",
        "timestamp": 1772942723
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this well scoped (the type of terms indexed by the number of free variables) or unscoped? We have confluence using locally nameless binding but would like to add a well scoped formalization as well.</p>",
        "id": 577973347,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1772944573
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I’m not quite sure the of the question here, are you asking about de Bruijn indexes vs de Bruijn levels? Otherwise I don’t think I did anything fancy so the answers probably no.</p>",
        "id": 577973538,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Carter",
        "timestamp": 1772944874
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am asking if the inductive type is like this (unscoped):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">var</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">abs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">app</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>or this (well scoped):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">var</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">abs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">app</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 577976979,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1772949007
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, it is the former.</p>",
        "id": 577978377,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Carter",
        "timestamp": 1772950538
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So prior to CSLib existing, I had done this style <a href=\"https://github.com/chenson2018/LeanScratch/tree/main/LeanScratch/DeBruijn\">here</a>. I wasn't very happy with how verbose shifting was, so I'd not ported to CSLib. But if you are interested in doing this better, particularly now that <code>grind</code> exists, I would be happy to review!</p>",
        "id": 577979222,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1772951570
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That code is probably not great for others reasons as well, so don't feel you have to follow it closely at all. Just something that could occasionally be helpful to reference.</p>",
        "id": 577979332,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1772951713
    }
